.TH CPU 1
.SH NAME
cpu \- execute a remote command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cpu
[
.B -1
]
[
.B -C
.I alg
]
[
.B -e
.I exportroot
]
.RI [ net\fB!\fP ] host
[
.I command
[
.IR arg ...
]
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Cpu
dials
.I host
(using network
.B tcp
if
.I net
is not given), exports a deliberately small local namespace and executes the given
.I command
on that machine. The local namespace is visible to the
command in
.BR /n/client ;
local device files are bound into the remote device directory.
If
.I command
is not given, then
.B /dis/sh
is run.
.PP
Before exporting any caller files, the client uses the version 2 request
protocol and waits for an acknowledgment from
.IR rstyxd (8).
EOF or any other response is a command failure.  This makes a rejected
transport choice or request observable to scripts instead of looking like a
successful command that printed nothing.
The
.B -1
option uses the original unacknowledged request protocol for an old server.
It should be used only for compatibility: that protocol cannot distinguish
early server rejection from successful empty output.
The acknowledgment reports that the server accepted and prepared the request;
it does not report the eventual exit status of the remote program.
.PP
The
.B -C
option sets the algorithm
to be used following authentication for digesting or encryption, to
.IR alg .
See 
.IR ssl (3)
for the supported algorithms.
The default is
.BR "aes_256_cbc sha256" :
the whole session is encrypted, and every record carries a SHA-256 MAC
so that tampering is detected.
.B "-C none"
asks for no
.IR ssl (3)
after authentication, for a server that offers nothing else;
a bare-metal InferNode's listener refuses it.
.PP
By default only the local
.B /dev
is exported.  The remote
.I rstyxd
binds it over its own, so a graphical command draws on the local screen
and reads the local mouse and keyboard.
.I Cpu
binds the draw device into
.B /dev
first if it is not already there.
The
.B -e
option explicitly exports
.I exportroot
instead; that directory must contain a
.B dev
subdirectory.  Use it to construct a narrow tree containing an additional
service such as
.BR wmexport (1).
Exporting
.B /
restores the historical behaviour but also delegates the caller's keyring,
home directory and host mounts, and is unsafe for an untrusted node.
See
.B docs/REMOTE-DESKTOP.md
for using a headless machine's desktop this way.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/cpu.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR dial (2),
.IR keyring-auth (2),
.IR security-auth (2)
.SH BUGS
Although the draw device files are visible
to the remote command, the original implementation
of Tk meant that windowing applications could not
receive events when
run remotely.
That has been fixed in this release, but
.I cpu
has not yet been updated to take advantage.
A later update will do that.
